+# 121 · A tie-break reordered to group its parameters by kind, proved equal under the stated reordering
+
+[← All examples](../README.md)
+
+| Verdict | Categories | Severity if shipped | Change | Verified unit | Approach |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| **✓ Verified: equivalent on every input** | functional, no-false-positive | none | refactor, behaviour change not intended | [`LastWriterWins#winner`](../../examples/apps/quorum/src/main/java/net/terminalvalue/quorum/replication/LastWriterWins.java) | [B9: Regression verification](../../lib/README.md) |
+
+> The last-writer-wins tie-break took its two writes interleaved, `(tsA, nodeA, tsB, nodeB)`; a developer groups the parameters by kind, timestamps first, and writes the body as the comparison chain the javadoc describes. A regression proof needs the same signature in both versions, and this one changed. The developer states the relation instead, `winner(tsA, nodeA, tsB, nodeB)` from the new parameters, and the proof is of the new method against the previous one called that way, on every input.
+
+Tags: [replicated-data](../README.md#tag-replicated-data), [agreement](../README.md#tag-agreement), [signature-change](../README.md#tag-signature-change)
+
+## The change
+
+Group the parameters by kind, `winner(tsA, tsB, nodeA, nodeB)`, so that a call reads as the two keys it compares, timestamps then node ids, and write the body as that comparison chain: `Long.compare` on the timestamps, `Integer.compare` on the node ids when they tie, the first write winning on a non-negative order. The two tests and the law in the properties file are updated to the new order.
+
+The method's signature changed, so the developer states how the previous version is called from the new parameters: `LastWriterWins#winner(tsA, nodeA, tsB, nodeB)`. The verifier proves that on every input, the new method agrees with the previous one called that way, or shows one on which it does not.
+
+**Before** ([`LastWriterWins.java`](../../examples/apps/quorum/src/main/java/net/terminalvalue/quorum/replication/LastWriterWins.java), line 12):
+
+```java
+    /**
+     * Which of two writes wins: {@code 0} for the first (timestamp {@code tsA} from node {@code nodeA}), {@code 1}
+     * for the second. Two replicas that see the writes in either order must agree, so for distinct writes
+     * {@code winner(a, b) == 1 - winner(b, a)}.
+     */
+    public static int winner(long tsA, int nodeA, long tsB, int nodeB) {
+        if (tsA != tsB) {
+            return tsA > tsB ? 0 : 1;
+        }
+        return nodeA >= nodeB ? 0 : 1;
+    }
+}
+```
+
+**After:**
+
+```java
+    /**
+     * Which of two writes wins: {@code 0} for the first (timestamp {@code tsA} from node {@code nodeA}), {@code 1}
+     * for the second. Two replicas that see the writes in either order must agree, so for distinct writes
+     * {@code winner(a, b) == 1 - winner(b, a)}. The order is the timestamps, then the node ids, as the parameters
+     * are grouped: the shape of {@code comparingLong(ts).thenComparingInt(node)}.
+     */
+    public static int winner(long tsA, long tsB, int nodeA, int nodeB) {
+        int order = Long.compare(tsA, tsB);
+        if (order == 0) {
+            order = Integer.compare(nodeA, nodeB);
+        }
+        return order >= 0 ? 0 : 1;
+    }
+}
+```
